.TH IFUP 1M "31 October 2026" "amix-base"
.SH NAME
ifup, ifdown \- bring network interfaces up or down
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B ifup
.B \-a
|
.I interface ...
.br
.B ifup \-n
[
.I interface ...
]
.br
.B ifdown
.B \-a
|
.I interface ...
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B ifup
sets up each
.I interface
the way its line in
.B /etc/inet/interfaces
says (see
.BR interfaces (4));
with
.BR \-a ,
every interface the installed network drivers find.
The boot runs
.B "ifup \-a"
from
.BR /etc/init.d/inetinit .
.PP
For each interface,
.B ifup
reads its station address with
.BR dlpi-mac (1M),
chooses its line (the line with that address, then the line with its name,
then the
.B *
line; with none, the interface is left down), links it under IP with
.B slink
if IP does not know it yet, and then follows the line's mode:
.TP
.B static
the address and mask are set with
.BR ifconfig .
.TP
.B dhcp
.B /usr/sbin/dhcpc
is asked for a lease and given
.B ifup_dhcpwait
seconds (from
.BR /etc/rc.conf ;
default 20).
If no lease comes in that time, or no DHCP client is installed, the
address on the line is used if it has one; otherwise the interface stays
down, and the DHCP client keeps trying.
.TP
.B off
nothing is done.
.PP
A static address and a DHCP lease are applied by the same steps, each only
when it is not already in place, so running
.B ifup
again changes nothing:
the address; the default route, from the line's
.B gw
or the lease's router (the first interface to set one keeps it); the
machine's name when the line says
.B name
(or the lease gives one under
.BR "hostname dhcp" );
this host's line in
.BR /etc/inet/hosts ,
for the interface that holds the default route (else the first one up);
and DNS.
DNS is turned on whenever a name server is known: the
.B dns
lines' servers and the lease's are written to
.B /etc/resolv.conf
(name servers only),
.B /usr/lib/libsocket.so
becomes
.BR libsockdns.so ,
.B /etc/netconfig
is taken from
.BR /etc/netconfig.DNS ,
and
.B /etc/domain
is emptied.
.PP
With
.BR \-n ,
.B ifup
only says, for each interface, which line applies and what it would do,
and lists every mistake in
.BR /etc/inet/interfaces .
.PP
.B ifdown
takes each
.I interface
down and removes the default route its line set.
An interface that holds a DHCP lease is released through the DHCP client.
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 when every interface named is up, or off as configured; 1 otherwise.
.SH FILES
.TP
.B /etc/inet/interfaces
how each interface is set up.
.TP
.B /etc/inet/link.d/*
the network drivers present, one line each.
.TP
.B /usr/lib/inet/ifup.subr
the steps
.BR ifup ,
.B ifdown
and the DHCP client's hook share.
.TP
.B /usr/lib/inet/dhcpc-hook
what the DHCP client runs to apply a lease.
.TP
.B /var/adm/rclog/ifup
the output of the boot's
.BR "ifup \-a" ;
each step is also in
.BR /var/adm/rc.log .
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR dlpi-mac (1M),
.BR interfaces (4),
.BR rc.conf (4)
.SH NOTES
.B ifdown
leaves the interface linked under IP:
.B slink
on this release can only unlink every interface at once.
